        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.544,
            0.735,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, the quadratic polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "ax^{2} + bx + c"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "no zero",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in this case."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.57,
            0.901,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, you can see geometrically that a quadratic polynomial can have either two distinct zeroes or two equal zeroes (i.e., one zero), or no zero. This also means that a polynomial of degree 2 has atmost two zeroes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.634,
            0.902,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, what do you expect the geometrical meaning of the zeroes of a cubic polynomial to be? Let us find out. Consider the cubic polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 3 } - 4 x ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . To see what the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = x ^ { 3 } - 4 \\underline { { x } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " looks like, let us list a few values of y corresponding to a few values for x as shown in Table 2.2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.751,
            0.792,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_caption",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.447,
                0.727,
                0.551,
                0.745
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 2.2"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.188,
                0.751,
                0.792,
                0.832
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_table_12.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>x</td><td>-2</td><td>-1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>y = x<sup>3</sup> - 4x</td><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>-3</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.859,
            0.9,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Locating the points of the table on a graph paper and drawing the graph, we see that the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = x ^ { 3 } - 4 x"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " actually looks like the one given in Fig. 2.6."
            }
          ]
        },
